<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amyclae</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amyklai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amyclae was an ancient town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, a few kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> valley. A Mycenaean centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, it was absorbed by Sparta, probably in the eighth century BCE, becoming the fifth of its villages. It was famous for the Amyclaeon, the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and Hyacinthus with a colossal statue of Apollo, where the Spartans celebrated the festival of the Hyacinthia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amyclae.jpg" alt="Amyclae" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rage against · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponeso_del_sur.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
